module nupp.util

--[[
Small general-purpose types that belong to no larger facility.

A member here is one type and the few functions that hand it out, reached by the
name of the type rather than by a module of its own: `nupp.util.Bitset` is a set
of bit positions, and `nupp.util.Store` is a bag of values indexed by typed keys.
Nothing here is a facade over a provider, and nothing here holds state a program
does not create itself.

```nupp
local set = new nupp.util.Bitset(128)
set:add(7)

local store = nupp.util.newStore()
const theme: nupp.util.Key<string> = nupp.util.newKey("app.theme")
store:set(theme, "dark")
```

Unlike [](nupp.mem), which keeps every member a module of its own, the types are
re-exported here, so reaching one loads both. That is affordable because both are
small and neither resolves a provider while it loads.

[](nupp.util.uuid) is the exception, and stays a module reached by its own name: it
resolves a host service and declares a native ABI, neither of which a program that
only wanted a bitset should carry.
]]

local bitset = require("nupp.util.internal.bitset")
local store = require("nupp.util.internal.store")

--- A growable set of bit positions counting from 0.
export type Bitset = bitset.Bitset

export const Bitset = bitset.Bitset

--- The bits one word of a [](Bitset) holds.
export const WORD_BITS = bitset.WORD_BITS

--- A typed identity for one value in a [](Store).
export type Key<T> = store.Key<T>

--- An independent typed bag indexed by keys.
export type Store = store.Store

--- The step of [](registeredKeys).
export type KeyStep = store.KeyStep

--- The step of [](Store.entries).
export type StoreStep = store.StoreStep

--- Registers a new key, optionally under a name.
export const newKey = store.newKey

--- Answers the key registered under a name, or nil.
export const findKey = store.findKey

--- Walks every registered key ascending by id.
export const registeredKeys = store.registeredKeys

--- Creates an empty store.
export const newStore = store.newStore
